  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a three-electrode electrochemical cell designed to operate under an inert atmosphere. A cylindrical vessel is filled with liquid electrolyte and closed by a lid holding several ports. Through the lid, an inert gas inlet feeds gas into the headspace, suggesting the solution is protected from air during measurements.\n\nThe main electrochemical setup includes a large working electrode immersed directly in the bulk solution, a slender counter electrode extending down into the cell, and a reference electrode positioned near the center. The reference electrode appears to be connected to the solution through a narrow inner tube or bridge that dips into the electrolyte, likely to provide a stable reference while limiting mixing. A small stir bar is shown at the bottom of the vessel, indicating the solution can be stirred during experiments.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the physical arrangement of the electrodes and gas handling in a standard laboratory electrochemical cell. The emphasis is on how the working, counter, and reference electrodes are spatially organized inside a sealed container so controlled electrochemical measurements can be made in solution while excluding reactive gases from the environment.",
